        "abstract": "Traffic congestion continues to pose significant challenges to urban mobility, costing the U.S. economy <inline-formula xmlns:mml=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"> <tex-math notation=\"LaTeX\">${\\$}$ </tex-math></inline-formula>224 billion annually and wasting 54 hours per commuter each year. Traditional traffic management systems, limited by static scheduling and insufficient data, are unable to adapt to real-time conditions effectively. This article explores the transformative potential of digital twin technology in addressing these inefficiencies. A digital twin—a virtual replica of a physical system—integrates real-time sensor data, artificial intelligence (AI)-driven analytics, and predictive modeling to dynamically optimize traffic flow, enhance urban planning, and prevent congestion before it occurs. Real-world implementations in cities such as Singapore, Dubai, and Los Angeles demonstrate the efficacy of digital twins in reducing congestion, optimizing traffic signals, and improving public transit systems. Beyond traffic management, this technology offers applications in energy optimization, emergency response, and healthcare infrastructure. This article delves into the mechanics of digital twins, provides case studies, and discusses challenges in adoption, such as cost and data integration. With advancements in AI, the Internet of Things (IoT), and 5G, digital twins are shaping the future of intelligent transportation and enabling smarter, more sustainable urban systems.",
